WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.189
Yesterday was a truly historic day.

00:00:03.189 --> 00:00:06.180
Donald Trump has become the third president

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:11.730
in the history of the United States to be
impeached, and this came at the conclusion

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:18.350
of really almost 12 hours of proceedings in
the house of representatives, including eight

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:20.199
hours of debate.

00:00:20.199 --> 00:00:25.090
The house of representatives impeach the president
of the United States on two articles.

00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:26.880
Article one is abuse of power.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.710
Article two is obstruction of Congress.

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:37.100
Now, there are both Republicans and some Democrats
who are pretending that this is no big deal

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:41.940
or they are skipping to, he's going to be
acquitted in the Senate and not removed.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:45.670
Therefore, this entire thing was a waste of
time.

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:51.120
That couldn't be the more, you couldn't find
a more incorrect place to start our analysis

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.190
of what took place yesterday.

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:56.460
So let's actually talk through it because
this is huge news.

00:00:56.460 --> 00:01:01.410
Only two presidents before Donald Trump have
ever been impeached, one in the 19th century

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:03.220
and another in the 20th century.

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:07.520
And now we have Donald Trump, president number
three in the 21st century.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:09.369
This is very rare.

00:01:09.369 --> 00:01:15.969
Andrew Johnson was impeached in 1868 was impeached
on 11 articles including violating the tenure

00:01:15.969 --> 00:01:23.420
of office act and Andrew Johnson was acquitted
in the eventual impeachment trial.

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:25.060
Go to the 20th century.

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:31.299
In 1998, bill Clinton was impeached for lying
under oath and for obstruction of justice

00:01:31.299 --> 00:01:37.039
related to a sexual harassment lawsuit that
was filed against him by Paula Jones and related,

00:01:37.039 --> 00:01:43.450
uh, to, uh, bill Clinton's testimony denying
a relationship he had had with white house

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:45.359
intern, Monica Lewinsky.

00:01:45.359 --> 00:01:50.390
Bill Clinton was also acquitted in the Senate
impeachment trial.

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:57.159
Now, Richard Nixon was not impeached in the
1970s he would have been and likely would

00:01:57.159 --> 00:01:58.779
have been convicted.

00:01:58.779 --> 00:02:03.099
But knowing this, knowing that the Republican
public had turned against Richard Nixon, knowing

00:02:03.099 --> 00:02:08.330
that Republicans in the Senate could no longer
stand by Richard Nixon knowing he would be

00:02:08.330 --> 00:02:09.769
impeached and convicted.

00:02:09.769 --> 00:02:13.569
He resigned on August 9th, 1974.

00:02:13.569 --> 00:02:16.689
An iconic moment in American political history.

00:02:16.689 --> 00:02:18.219
Quick reminder of that

00:02:18.219 --> 00:02:25.490
on the great issue with peace abroad and prosperity
without inflation at home.

00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:30.860
Therefore, I shall resign the presidency effective
at noon tomorrow.

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:36.620
Vice president Ford will be sworn in as president
at that hour.

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:37.620
So

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:39.090
a huge, huge deal.

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:42.489
Even Nixon was not impeached but resigned.

00:02:42.489 --> 00:02:44.519
Let's go to video from yesterday.

00:02:44.519 --> 00:02:48.230
Last night, uh, we were streaming at the time,
I think it must've been about eight, 8:15

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:53.090
PM Eastern time, a video from the house of
representatives, speaker of the house, Nancy

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:55.870
Pelosi announcing the impeachment.

00:02:55.870 --> 00:03:00.180
You will also, if you're watching notice her
visibly quiet, the applause and cheering that

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:06.530
broke out from the democratic side, clearly
wanting, uh, not to make this seem like something

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:09.739
you would cheer about because it is very serious
quite frankly.

00:03:09.739 --> 00:03:14.420
Here she is announcing article number one
impeachment of Donald Trump on this vote.

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:16.010
The yeas are 230.

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:29.049
The nays are one 97 present is one article,
one is adopted.

00:03:29.049 --> 00:03:34.280
The question is aren't adoption of articles.

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:38.889
And then very shortly thereafter, after a
five minute plus, a couple extra minute vote

00:03:38.889 --> 00:03:43.530
on article two, obstruction of Congress, Nancy
Pelosi speaker of the house announcing that

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:46.349
Donald Trump has also been impeached on article
number two

00:03:46.349 --> 00:03:47.349
on this vote.

00:03:47.349 --> 00:03:53.230
The A's are 229 the nays are one 98.

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:54.980
Present is one.

00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:57.560
Article two is adopted.

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:01.909
The votes were all most almost along party
lines.

00:04:01.909 --> 00:04:07.030
230 in total voting for impeachment on article
one because of vacancies in the house.

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:10.040
Instead of two 18 to impeach, they needed
only two 16.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:18.209
They got two 30 on article two, uh, almost
the same numbers, 229 voting to impeach one

00:04:18.209 --> 00:04:24.620
former Republican who is now an independent
Justin Amash voted to impeach on both counts.

00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:29.849
You then had two Democrats, Collin Peterson
of Minnesota who some know of as a dyno Democrat

00:04:29.849 --> 00:04:35.360
in name only, Colin Peterson of Minnesota,
not voting to impeach Jefferson van drew of

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:37.520
New Jersey voting not to impeach.

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:41.520
And van drew is um, this member who's made
headlines over the last few days.

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:47.050
He's expected to switch from being a Democrat
to a Republican very, very soon.

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:51.910
And then you had Jared golden of Maine who
split his vote saying yes on article one no

00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:53.530
on article two.

00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:58.639
And that's why there were 230 votes in favor
on article one.

00:04:58.639 --> 00:05:02.830
Uh, only 229 votes in favor on article two.

00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:09.000
Then you had a one individual who voted present
and that was, um, there's this Hawaii Congress

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:15.349
woman she's running, um, technically running
for president, uh, Tulsi Gabard who is not

00:05:15.349 --> 00:05:17.340
seeking reelection for her seat.

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:21.500
She took no position voting merely present
on both articles.

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:25.289
She said, quote, I could not in good conscience
vote against impeachment because I believe

00:05:25.289 --> 00:05:27.060
Donald Trump is guilty of wrongdoing.

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:32.060
I also could not in good conscience vote for
impeachment because removal of a sitting president

00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:34.500
must not be the culmination of a partisan
process.

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:38.870
Now what's complete nonsense about that is
that there's never an even split in Congress

00:05:38.870 --> 00:05:40.270
between the parties.

00:05:40.270 --> 00:05:43.910
So by definition you have 435, it's an odd
number.

00:05:43.910 --> 00:05:49.430
It's always a partisan process in the sense
that it's led by one party.

00:05:49.430 --> 00:05:53.050
Uh, and by that logic, she could never vote
to impeach anyone.

00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:57.970
Now, I guess what she's saying is she would've
preferred the process, involve more Republicans,

00:05:57.970 --> 00:06:03.740
but that's a completely unrealistic expectation
in the hyper-partisan Fox news era of politics.

00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:06.930
I don't want to waste too much time on Tulsi
Gabard.

00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:07.970
Who cares what she does?

00:06:07.970 --> 00:06:08.970
Okay.

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:10.669
She decided to just ride the middle.

00:06:10.669 --> 00:06:15.370
I do think it is yet another signal that she
may be planning a third party run for president,

00:06:15.370 --> 00:06:18.590
which could have its own series of implications.

00:06:18.590 --> 00:06:20.610
She'll be gone for the house in January.

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:21.610
Okay.

00:06:21.610 --> 00:06:24.509
I don't think I need to say too much more
about that.

00:06:24.509 --> 00:06:26.830
Now, here is Trump.

00:06:26.830 --> 00:06:30.270
I'm talking back in 2008 with Wolf Blitzer.

00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:32.449
You know what took place yesterday was a big
deal.

00:06:32.449 --> 00:06:37.000
It was carried out by serious people, Nancy
Pelosi speaker of the house.

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:42.350
You had uh, uh, um, uh, Adam Schiff in the
house intelligence committee.

00:06:42.350 --> 00:06:44.860
You had Jerry Nadler in the house judiciary
committee.

00:06:44.860 --> 00:06:47.480
These are serious people doing serious work.

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:48.810
Here's video of Donald Trump.

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:54.780
Back in 2008 telling Wolf Blitzer, he finds
Nancy Pelosi impressive and would like to

00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:57.439
have seen her actually do more on impeachment
in times past.

00:06:57.439 --> 00:06:59.479
This is a classic video instantly.

00:06:59.479 --> 00:07:06.259
Nancy Pelosi, the speaker, well, you know
when she first got in and was named speaker,

00:07:06.259 --> 00:07:08.031
I met her and I'm very impressed by her.

00:07:08.031 --> 00:07:09.680
I think she's a very impressive person.

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:17.610
I like her a lot, but I was surprised that
she didn't do more in terms of Bush and going

00:07:17.610 --> 00:07:18.610
after Bush.

00:07:18.610 --> 00:07:23.490
It was almost, it just seemed like she was
going to really look to impeach Bush and get

00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:27.810
him out of office, which personally I think
would have been a wonderful thing for the

00:07:27.810 --> 00:07:30.800
war, for the war because of the conduct.

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:31.800
We lied.

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:39.979
He got us into the war with lies and I mean,
look at the trouble bill Clinton got into

00:07:39.979 --> 00:07:46.280
with something that was totally unimportant
and they tried to impeach him, which was nonsense.

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:51.360
And yet Bush got us into this horrible war
with lies by lying, by saying they had weapons

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:56.199
of mass destruction by saying all sorts of
things that turned out not to be true.

00:07:56.199 --> 00:07:57.199
So

00:07:57.199 --> 00:08:02.129
Nancy Pelosi has done it and this is already
significant no matter what anybody tells you,

00:08:02.129 --> 00:08:03.259
this is a huge deal.

00:08:03.259 --> 00:08:05.189
This is a historic day.

00:08:05.189 --> 00:08:08.139
Now let's talk about the next step.

00:08:08.139 --> 00:08:14.169
The next step is a trial in the Senate without
regard to the likely outcome of the Senate

00:08:14.169 --> 00:08:15.689
trial.

00:08:15.689 --> 00:08:22.219
Impeachment is a serious constitutional responsibility
given to the house and Senate and the house

00:08:22.219 --> 00:08:23.319
did its job.

00:08:23.319 --> 00:08:27.199
And I know that there are lots of people and
they're emailing me and tweeting me saying,

00:08:27.199 --> 00:08:29.360
Trump's going to be acquitted in the Senate.

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:31.199
This is all a waste of time.

00:08:31.199 --> 00:08:36.360
The house is the, it has a role to play in
this process and it is to determine whether

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:38.840
articles of impeachment are appropriate.

00:08:38.840 --> 00:08:40.450
They did their job.

00:08:40.450 --> 00:08:41.790
Now I agree with you.

00:08:41.790 --> 00:08:45.850
The most likely outcome of the trial in the
Senate is Trump being acquitted because Republicans

00:08:45.850 --> 00:08:48.950
control the Senate and we'll go through the
math in a moment.

00:08:48.950 --> 00:08:54.530
But you sometimes just have to do the right
thing because it's the right thing to do,

00:08:54.530 --> 00:08:57.680
even if it won't have the immediate effect
that you might want.

00:08:57.680 --> 00:09:03.280
Imagine not doing this and then Trump gets
himself reelected and then in five or 10 years

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:06.640
you realize why, why didn't I vote to impeach?

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:08.510
It was the right thing to do.

00:09:08.510 --> 00:09:12.310
It might have prevented Trump from getting
reelected, although it might not.

00:09:12.310 --> 00:09:13.900
Uh Oh right.

00:09:13.900 --> 00:09:16.180
We calculated we were going to lose in the
Senate.

00:09:16.180 --> 00:09:17.970
No, that's not how we do things.

00:09:17.970 --> 00:09:22.350
Trump has been daring anyone to hold them
accountable and he's basically not been held

00:09:22.350 --> 00:09:23.350
accountable.

00:09:23.350 --> 00:09:26.820
He has now been impeached and is one of only
three presidents in history to have suffered

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:27.820
that fate.

00:09:27.820 --> 00:09:32.350
It's the right step, even if he is not going
to be convicted in the Senate.

00:09:32.350 --> 00:09:35.550
So let's now get to the analysis of the Senate.

00:09:35.550 --> 00:09:39.860
Is it possible that Donald Trump will be convicted
in the Senate?

00:09:39.860 --> 00:09:40.860
Sure.

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:41.860
It's possible.

00:09:41.860 --> 00:09:44.210
The math is you need two third support.

00:09:44.210 --> 00:09:45.770
There's a hundred senators.

00:09:45.770 --> 00:09:52.020
Two thirds is 66 and two thirds, meaning 67
senators need to vote to convict.

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:54.270
In order for the president to be removed.

00:09:54.270 --> 00:09:56.890
We have 45 Democrats in the Senate.

00:09:56.890 --> 00:10:02.060
There are two independence, Bernie Sanders
and Angus King who caucus with the Democrats.

00:10:02.060 --> 00:10:05.670
That's like 47 votes probably for conviction.

00:10:05.670 --> 00:10:07.020
Joe mansion.

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:12.270
The most conservative democratic Senator,
uh, might not vote to convict.

00:10:12.270 --> 00:10:13.270
We don't know.

00:10:13.270 --> 00:10:17.530
But for our purposes today, let's assume we
have 47 votes in the Senate.

00:10:17.530 --> 00:10:19.510
That means you need 20 Republicans.

00:10:19.510 --> 00:10:25.870
Uh, Chris Murphy, a Senator from Connecticut
says he thinks that there's about five Republican

00:10:25.870 --> 00:10:29.150
senators that would consider voting, uh, for
conviction.

00:10:29.150 --> 00:10:33.760
Uh, bill weld, former Republican governor
here in Massachusetts who's running a pretty

00:10:33.760 --> 00:10:38.040
flimsy, uh, campaign, but a campaign nonetheless,
a primary campaign against Donald Trump.

00:10:38.040 --> 00:10:42.620
Bill weld says he thinks there's about five
Republicans that would consider voting to

00:10:42.620 --> 00:10:43.620
convict.

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:44.950
That won't be enough.

00:10:44.950 --> 00:10:46.530
Even if that's true, it won't be enough.

00:10:46.530 --> 00:10:49.890
So is it likely that Trump will be convicted,
convicted?

00:10:49.890 --> 00:10:51.010
Absolutely not.

00:10:51.010 --> 00:10:54.810
The Republican public and voters don't want
it.

00:10:54.810 --> 00:10:58.730
Republican senators I don't think would even
really consider it unfortunately unless the

00:10:58.730 --> 00:11:03.530
electorate wanted it because it would be too
politically disadvantageous or at least potentially

00:11:03.530 --> 00:11:04.530
risky.

00:11:04.530 --> 00:11:10.910
Um, but the idea would be if Chris Murphy
and bill weld are correct, if there are a

00:11:10.910 --> 00:11:15.820
handful of Republicans willing to vote for
conviction, maybe that would spread like a

00:11:15.820 --> 00:11:22.070
virus and if you know, I don't think it's
going to happen, but if they decided that

00:11:22.070 --> 00:11:29.030
there would be a secret ballot where it wouldn't
be known which senators vote, which way you

00:11:29.030 --> 00:11:33.350
might be able to enough momentum where you
get 20 Republicans that say let's convict.

00:11:33.350 --> 00:11:34.650
I think it is very unlikely.

00:11:34.650 --> 00:11:39.910
If I had to handicap it, I would say it's
a roughly two to 4% chance that something

00:11:39.910 --> 00:11:41.580
like that happens.

00:11:41.580 --> 00:11:43.000
What are the logistics of the trial?

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:45.010
Number one, when will the trial be?

00:11:45.010 --> 00:11:50.040
It's unclear because it was presumably going
to be in January upon the return from holiday

00:11:50.040 --> 00:11:51.040
break.

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:55.020
But we have just heard from Nancy Pelosi and
we'll deal with this story in full later on

00:11:55.020 --> 00:12:00.010
in the program, that she is going to withhold
the articles from the Senate until such time

00:12:00.010 --> 00:12:05.600
that she believes there will be what she considers
to be a fair hearing and a fair trial.

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:10.850
That may never happen, but uh, the January
date is now certainly a question Mark.

00:12:10.850 --> 00:12:13.120
How long will the impeachment trial last?

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:14.140
We don't know.

00:12:14.140 --> 00:12:18.710
It would generally be expected to run somewhere
between two and four weeks.

00:12:18.710 --> 00:12:20.110
Will it be televised?

00:12:20.110 --> 00:12:21.120
I don't know.

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:26.710
Um, I did a bunch of research on this today
and there is no hard and fast rule about what

00:12:26.710 --> 00:12:28.149
has to happen.

00:12:28.149 --> 00:12:34.490
It would be part of the negotiations that,
um, the house leadership would have with Senate

00:12:34.490 --> 00:12:37.460
leadership as they move articles of impeachment
in that direction.

00:12:37.460 --> 00:12:41.871
Now, unfortunately, Donald Trump will also
get a say because Mitch McConnell, the Senate

00:12:41.871 --> 00:12:46.120
majority leader has already said he will be
in total coordination with the white house

00:12:46.120 --> 00:12:49.920
about how the impeachment trial will be carried
out.

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:50.920
That's not good.

00:12:50.920 --> 00:12:54.450
Uh, will this help Trump win in 2020?

00:12:54.450 --> 00:12:56.570
That is the major political question.

00:12:56.570 --> 00:13:00.840
If Trump is convicted, he's out and he will
not be reelected.

00:13:00.840 --> 00:13:05.180
Of course, if Trump is acquitted, there are
two ways that it could go.

00:13:05.180 --> 00:13:10.780
One is his acquittal is successfully used
as look at this witch hunt.

00:13:10.780 --> 00:13:12.220
I am a martyr.

00:13:12.220 --> 00:13:15.750
I am the most unfairly treated president in
history, which he's been tweeting about and

00:13:15.750 --> 00:13:21.070
we'll get to later and it reinvigorates his
supporters, uh, and increases right-wing voter

00:13:21.070 --> 00:13:23.310
turnout and rockets him to reelection.

00:13:23.310 --> 00:13:28.070
That is a plausible, and I would say even
realistic political risk to this process.

00:13:28.070 --> 00:13:34.340
The other would be if the timing and the setup
of impeachment, uh, it gets delayed a little

00:13:34.340 --> 00:13:35.340
bit.

00:13:35.340 --> 00:13:38.240
So it starts overlapping with when we start
getting into the general election, maybe over

00:13:38.240 --> 00:13:44.210
the summer, hypothetically, if it is televised,
if Trump is constantly going crazy publicly,

00:13:44.210 --> 00:13:49.060
if it becomes subject matter of the debates
between Donald Trump and the eventual democratic

00:13:49.060 --> 00:13:52.650
nominee, it could certainly end up hurting
him.

00:13:52.650 --> 00:13:55.990
It's a question Mark, but there are major
political risks with this doesn't change that

00:13:55.990 --> 00:13:57.930
it's the right thing to do.

00:13:57.930 --> 00:14:02.550
Lastly, would there be violence if Donald
Trump were convicted and removed?

00:14:02.550 --> 00:14:06.910
Uh, I believe that there would they, uh, on
the right, the Trump has to have been throwing

00:14:06.910 --> 00:14:12.000
around terms like a civil war picking sides.

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:16.360
And uh, well, you know, we also heard from
